course: dictionary
lesson: 6133
title: Migration: a journey, not a holiday
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A migration is a big journey from one place to another, and it is often repeated. Animals may do it every year to find food, warmth or a place to breed. «Migration means people moving to live in another place, inside their country or abroad.» So a migration is not just any trip: it is a move, usually to live somewhere new or to follow the seasons.
explain: Context helps you spot the meaning. «The Arctic tern has the longest migration, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year.» Flying, going to the Antarctic and coming back each year all point to a long, regular journey. Ask yourself: is this a one-off trip, or a move that happens again and again?
explain: Migration is also a word you will see in phrases such as «bird migration», «the great migration» and «migration route». A route is the path a journey follows. Put them together and a bird migration route is the path birds follow on their long journeys each year.
fact: Migration means a large movement from one place to another.
fact: For animals, migration is often seasonal, to find food, warmth or a place to breed.
fact: «Migration» can also mean people moving to live in another place, inside their country or abroad.
fact: The Arctic tern makes one of the longest migrations of any animal, flying from the Arctic to Antarctica and back each year.
fact: Net migration is the number of people who arrived minus the number who left.
exercise: Birds such as swallows fly south for the winter. What is this movement called? | Evaporation | *Migration | Digestion
exercise: «Migration means people moving to live in another place, inside their country or abroad.» Which is an example of migration? | A man reads a book in the library | *A family moves to a new city in another country to find work | A girl walks across the road to school
exercise: «The Arctic tern has the longest migration, flying from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year.» Where does the tern fly to? | Scotland | Brazil | *The Antarctic | The desert
test: «Net migration is the number of people who arrived minus the number who left.» If 500 people arrived and 300 left, what is net migration? | 300 | 800 | 500 | *200
test: In «the great migration», what is being described? | *A large movement of animals or people from one place to another | A party in a big hall | A school trip to the seaside
exam: «The Arctic tern sees more daylight than any other animal, because it follows the summer from one end of the Earth to the other.» What does this tell you about the tern's migration? | *It follows the summer, so it moves a very long way | It sleeps in the dark for months | It lives underground all year | It never leaves its nest
